    "lawCode": "Add §139-a, amd §§375 & 1144-a, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5397\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to the display of amber and blue lights\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo allow tow trucks and roadside service vehicles to display\nrear-facing amber and blue lights while engaged in a hazardous\noperation.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Adds a new section 139-a to the vehicle and traffic law to\ndefine roadside service vehicle as a vehicle that is used for the\nproviding of fuel, jump starts, tire changes, lockout services, minor\nrepairs and similar services to motorists and, if equipped to do so,\nto push disabled vehicles from traffic lanes.\n\nSection 2. Adds a new sub-paragraph c to paragraph 4 of subdivision 41\nof section 375 of the vehicle and traffic law to allow tow trucks and\nroadside service vehicles to display amber and blue lights when\nengaged in a hazardous operation.\n\nSection 3. Amends paragraph (b) of section 1144-a of the vehicle and\ntraffic law to require motorists to slow down and move over for hazard\nvehicles displaying amber and blue lights (tow trucks and roadside\nservice vehicles).\n\nSection 4. The act shall take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn October of 2016, two tow truck operators and a NYS Thruway\nmaintenance worker were killed while providing roadside services in\nNew York. A third tow truck operator was killed in December and\nanother sustained a serious brain injury in November that caused him\nto lose his business. They join approximately seven other tow truck\noperators who have been killed in recent years and numerous others who\nhave been injured while providing roadside services. There have also\nbeen numerous roadside accidents that have resulted in damage to tow\ntrucks, roadside service vehicles and emergency vehicles that do not\nresult in personal injuries.\n\nTow truck and roadside service vehicle drivers suffer a far greater\npercentage of roadside injuries and deaths than police and other first\nresponders to accident scenes. For one thing, there are usually\npersons on hand to control traffic while others minister to the\ninjured and clear accident scenes. Tow truck and roadside service\ndrivers, on the other hand, are often acting alone.\n\nThe color of flashing lights is another factor. Law enforcement\nofficers are quick to acknowledge that motorists simply don't respect\nyellow lights. More importantly, scientific studies have demonstrated\nthat blue lights are far more visible than any other color.\nHopefully, this bill will provide tow truck and roadside service\ndrivers with greater protection while performing hazardous work\nalongside our state's highways.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew legislation\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone.\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
